Depth-0 recall exceeds 98\% in all 9 cells; among generated depths, d2 consistently yields the lowest recall (46--73\%), reflecting overlap with both adjacent classes (full per-class recall in Appendix~\ref{app:per_class}).

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Feature Consistency Across the Grid}
\label{sec:atlas:features}

Despite the 15.8~pp accuracy spread, a small feature family dominates universally.
\texttt{p90\_ppl} ranks top-2 by Gini importance in all 9 cells; \texttt{var\_surprisal} in 8/9 under SHAP (7/9 Gini; full per-cell rankings in Table~\ref{tab:feature_importance_full}, Appendix~\ref{app:feature_importance}).
Cross-model feature-rank Spearman correlation averages $\bar{\rho} = 0.868$: models agree on which features carry depth information.
Feature ablation confirms concentration: top-2 features alone reach 91.1\% of full accuracy; top-5 achieve 97.6\%.
VIF analysis reveals 14--16 of 19 features with VIF${>}$10, placing effective dimensionality at ${\approx}$4--5; forward selection confirms the dominant pair (\texttt{p90\_ppl} first in 6/9 cells, \texttt{var\_surprisal} in 3/9).
The hierarchy shifts with depth---\texttt{var\_surprisal} dominates at shallow boundaries while tail perplexity statistics take over deeper, consistent with model-collapse dynamics~\cite{shumailov2024_nature} (importance heatmap in Figure~\ref{fig:atlas_heatmap_full}, Appendix~\ref{app:feature_importance}).

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Monotonicity and EDD Analysis}
\label{sec:atlas:edd}

Depth features exhibit strong monotonic trends: perplexity tail statistics decrease with depth (Spearman $\rho = -1.0$ across cell-level medians) while repetition features increase (trajectories in Figure~\ref{fig:feature_trajectory_full}, Appendix~\ref{app:topk_trajectories}).
Inter-depth distances are non-uniform: d0-vs-d1 AUC exceeds 0.99 in all cells, whereas d2-vs-d3 ranges from 0.69 to 0.89.

We extend to depth $d{=}5$ for all three models under nucleus sampling, training dedicated binary RF classifiers (500 trees) for each adjacent pair (Eq.~\ref{eq:edd}).
Figure~\ref{fig:auc_decay} shows the AUC decay: Pythia-1.4B decays monotonically from 0.999 (d0v1) to 0.683 (d4v5), with the steepest drop between d1v2 and d2v3.
The d3-vs-d4 AUC clusters near $T{=}0.75$ across all three models (0.750--0.784), while d4-vs-d5 uniformly falls below 0.70, establishing a consistent depth-4 decay boundary.
At $T{=}0.75$, 7/9 atlas cells maintain $\mathrm{EDD} \geq 3$ on the primary benchmark; at $T{=}0.80$, 5/9 (full threshold sensitivity in Table~\ref{tab:edd_sensitivity_full}, Appendix~\ref{app:edd_sensitivity}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_auc_decay.pdf}
\caption{Binary pairwise AUC decay curves for three models (nucleus sampling, depths 0--5). Horizontal dashed lines mark $T \in \{0.65, 0.70, 0.75, 0.80\}$. All three curves converge below 0.70 at d4-vs-d5. Shaded bands: bootstrap 95\% CIs.}
\label{fig:auc_decay}
\end{figure}

EDD uses binary-pair classifiers to avoid decision boundary dilution: in Pythia nucleus, d2-vs-d3 AUC degrades from 0.804 (4-class) to 0.689 (6-class) despite identical data.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Strategy-Dependent Trajectories}
\label{sec:atlas:strategy}

The three decoding strategies induce qualitatively distinct degradation trajectories.
Nucleus ($p{=}0.95$) produces the most consistent accuracy (78.2--83.3\%) and serves as the reference.
Temperature ($\tau{=}0.9$) yields the highest accuracy in 2/3 models because uniform logit scaling accelerates convergence to an attractor distribution~\cite{shumailov2024_nature}, amplifying the fingerprint---but creating a boundary reversal risk where temperature depth-$N$ features approximate nucleus/top-$k$ depth-$(N{+}1)$ features (\S\ref{sec:generalization}).
Top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) produces the lowest accuracy (69.6--76.6\%) because hard vocabulary truncation removes depth-discriminative low-probability tokens, though feature direction is preserved (cross-model $\rho = 0.811$).
The strategy effect (8.4~pp range) exceeds the model effect (7.1~pp).
