\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We evaluate generalization along seven axes: four below---classifier choice (\S\ref{sec:gen:baselines}), cross-model transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossmodel}), cross-strategy transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}), and cross-scale (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossscale})---and three in the Appendix (end-to-end learning, temperature robustness, cross-boundary transfer).

\subsection{Baseline Comparison}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} evaluates RF, XGBoost, and LightGBM on the same 19 features across three atlas cells.
Per-cell spread is $\leq$0.4pp, establishing a feature-determined ceiling independent of the learning algorithm.
CORAL ordinal regression \cite{coral2020} and DivEye \cite{diveye2026} match RF within 0.1\,pp ($p>0.05$); all feature-based methods significantly outperform CE-MLP (74.9\%) and logistic regression (75.0\%).
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); two zero-shot LLMs---DeepSeek V3.2 (36.2\%, 95\% CI $[32.0, 40.4]$) and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (30.4\%, $[26.5, 34.6]$)---also fail, with an accuracy gap exceeding 42\,pp vs.\ RF.
Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix} shows d0 near-perfectly identified; errors concentrate at d2/d3.

